A||||5||Gold-86SE||? for you guys that have done restore work - kinda long||||1||
Z||000000||Gold-86SE||08-08-2000||12:37 PM||||<BR>Except for the dash and steering column, I have pulled the interior out of my 85 GT.  And the metal floor board on the passenger side is giving me pause for thought.<P>I am seeing bubbling of the paint, which I believe indicates beginning of rust.  I was thinking of sanding these areas out of the floor board and then repainting it.  What do you guys think?<P>Also, regarding the floor board.  The metal plates that clip into the molded floor board openings have had their rubber seals come lose.  This has allowed water into the passenger compartment that has contributed to the interior rust mentioned above.  My question is, after I clean up the opening, how did you guys reattach the rubber seal to the plate covering?<BR><P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)||||reg||1
Z||000001||pollock||08-08-2000||01:37 PM||||Just silicone it up.  You may not even need the rubber seal.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodrv6||08-08-2000||02:37 PM||||Since you've gone through the trouble of pulling the carpets out, I would definatly sand and repaint the floor pans if there is any sign of bubbling paint!  I had the same problem with water coming in around the hole covers. Pollock is right--silicone sealer works perfectly to seal them up.  Make sure you use enough to get a complete seal.  Looks aren't important here- the carpet will cover it up.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE||||reg||6
Z||000003||theogre||08-08-2000||03:17 PM||||Use "rustolium" paint & primer on the floor board. It will hold up better if it gets damp again.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfixit58||08-08-2000||04:32 PM||||You need to make sure you kill the rust before you try to paint it over. Look for a rust converter on the shelf of your local auto parts store. Just sanding it will remove the surface rust but if there is pitting, it will eventually come back. For the floorboards, Rustolium will work OK. <P>For the floorboard plug, you can use the silicon as mentioned or body caulk. The advantage to body caulk is that it's paintable. Paint doesn't stick to silicon very well.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000005||LarryB||08-08-2000||10:47 PM||||You can get rust neutralizer that you paint on or you can get the "Naval Jelly" brand in a spray version where you spray a coat then spray on a 2nd coat within 20 minutes.   Because both need surface rust to work properly they don't work well if you sand down to the bare metal.  They also need to cure for at least 24 hours before you paint over them or cover them up.<P>Rustoleum sells a version of their spray paint with the rust inhibitor/neutralizer in it now, I've been using it and it works pretty well, it works good for fixing up your battery tray as well. <BR><p>[This message has been edited by LarryB (edited 08-08-2000).]||||reg||1
